    <h1>Why Does pH Affect Enzyme Activity?</h1>

    <p class="subtitle">
        The molecular mechanisms linking pH, protein structure and enzyme catalysis
    </p>

    <p class="author">
        Independent Literature Review<br>
        Raees · Biological Sciences · Imperial College London
    </p>
        <h2>Introduction</h2>

    <p>
        Enzymes are biological catalysts that reduce the activation energy
        required for reactions to occur. Their activity is influenced by several
        environmental factors, including temperature, pH, enzyme concentration,
        substrate concentration and inhibitors. This review examines how changes
        in pH affect enzyme activity through alterations in protein structure,
        active-site chemistry, substrate binding and catalytic activity.
        Particular attention is given to how changes in pH affect amino acids in
        the active site, as well as how extreme pH can change the shape and
        stability of enzymes.
    </p>


    <h2>Protein Structure and Enzyme Function</h2>

    <p>
        Proteins are polymers composed of amino acids linked by peptide bonds.
        The sequence of amino acids determines the primary structure of a protein,
        which contributes to the way the protein folds into its specific
        three-dimensional shape. This three-dimensional shape is important for
        enzymes because it determines the structure of the active site and
        therefore affects how the enzyme interacts with its substrate.
    </p>

    <p>
        Interactions between amino-acid side chains help stabilise the
        three-dimensional structure of a protein, allowing it to maintain the
        specific shape required for its function. In enzymes, this structure is
        particularly important because it allows the active site to maintain the
        correct and complementary shape for binding to its substrate. Changes to
        these interactions can therefore affect the shape of the active site and
        ultimately reduce enzyme activity.
    </p>
        <h2>The Active Site</h2>

    <p>
        The active site is a specialised region of an enzyme where the substrate
        binds. The shape and chemical properties of the active site are important
        because they determine which substrates can bind effectively to the
        enzyme. When a substrate enters the active site, interactions between
        the substrate and enzyme can cause small changes in the shape of the
        active site. This is known as the induced-fit model and helps position
        the substrate correctly for the reaction to occur.
    </p>

    <p>
        Because the active site contains specific amino-acid residues, changes
        in their chemical properties can affect how well the enzyme binds to its
        substrate and carries out the reaction. The protonation states of
        active-site residues can be particularly important because catalytic
        reactions may require specific residues to be in particular protonation
        states. This provides one way in which changes in pH can influence enzyme
        activity.
    </p>
        <h2>pH and Amino-Acid Protonation</h2>

    <p>
        pH is a measure related to the concentration of hydrogen ions (H⁺) in
        a solution. The pH scale typically ranges from 0 to 14, with lower
        values representing more acidic conditions and higher values
        representing more alkaline conditions. A pH of 7 is considered neutral.
        The pH scale is logarithmic, meaning that a change of one pH unit
        represents a tenfold change in hydrogen-ion concentration.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is particularly important because changes in hydrogen-ion
        concentration can affect the chemical properties of amino acids that
        make up the enzyme. Amino acids with ionisable side chains can change
        their protonation states depending on the pH of their surroundings.
        The protonation state of these residues can influence protein stability,
        interactions and enzyme activity.
    </p>

    <p>
        Protonation is the process by which a molecule or ion gains a proton
        (H⁺), while deprotonation is the loss of a proton. Some amino-acid
        side chains contain functional groups that can gain or lose protons
        depending on the pH of their surroundings. When an amino acid gains
        or loses a proton, its electrical charge can change. This can affect
        its interactions with other amino acids and therefore influence the
        structure and function of the enzyme.
    </p>

    <p>
        If the affected amino acid is located within the active site, a change
        in its charge can alter how well the enzyme interacts with its
        substrate or participates in the reaction. In some enzymes, catalytic
        residues must be in a particular protonation state for the reaction
        to occur effectively.
    </p>
        <h2>pH and Enzyme Kinetics</h2>

    <p>
        Enzyme kinetics is the study of the rate of enzyme-catalysed reactions
        and how this rate changes under different conditions. The rate of
        enzyme-catalysed reactions depends on factors such as substrate
        concentration, enzyme concentration and environmental conditions such
        as temperature and pH.
    </p>

    <p>
        The relationship between substrate concentration and reaction rate can
        be described using Michaelis–Menten kinetics. Two important parameters
        are the maximum reaction velocity (Vmax) and the Michaelis constant
        (Km). Vmax represents the maximum rate reached when the enzyme is
        saturated with substrate, while Km is the substrate concentration at
        which the reaction rate reaches half of Vmax.
    </p>

    <p>
        Changes in pH can alter enzyme kinetics by changing the protonation
        states of amino-acid residues involved in substrate binding or
        catalysis. As a result, changes in pH can affect how effectively an
        enzyme binds to its substrate and how efficiently it converts the
        substrate into product.
    </p>

    <p>
        The effect of pH is different for each enzyme. This is because enzymes
        have different structures and different amino acids in their active
        sites. Changes in pH can affect these amino acids, which can change how
        well the enzyme binds to its substrate and carries out the reaction.
        Therefore, pH can affect Vmax and Km differently depending on the
        enzyme.
    </p>
        <h2>Why Enzymes Have Different Optimum pH Values</h2>

    <p>
        Different enzymes work best at different pH values. The pH at which
        an enzyme has its highest activity is called its optimum pH.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is because enzymes have different structures and different
        amino acids in their active sites. These amino acids can gain or lose
        hydrogen ions depending on the pH. This can change their charge and
        affect how they interact with the substrate.
    </p>

    <p>
        For example, pepsin works best in acidic conditions, such as those
        found in the stomach. In contrast, trypsin works best in more alkaline
        conditions in the small intestine. These differences demonstrate that
        enzyme activity depends on the specific structure and chemical
        properties of each enzyme.
    </p>

    <p>
        At the optimum pH, the structure and active site of an enzyme are
        suited to carrying out the reaction effectively. If the pH moves away
        from this optimum, changes to amino-acid charges and interactions can
        reduce enzyme activity. Therefore, the optimum pH of an enzyme depends
        on its structure, the amino acids involved in its active site and the
        environment in which it normally functions.
    </p>
        <h2>Extreme pH and Enzyme Denaturation</h2>

    <p>
        When the pH moves far away from an enzyme's optimum pH, enzyme activity
        can decrease significantly. Extreme pH can change the charge of
        amino-acid side chains within an enzyme. This can disrupt interactions
        between amino acids that help maintain the enzyme's three-dimensional
        structure.
    </p>

    <p>
        As a result, the structure of the enzyme can change and the active site
        may no longer have the correct shape to bind its substrate effectively.
    </p>

    <p>
        If these changes are severe, the enzyme can become denatured.
        Denaturation involves a loss of the protein's normal three-dimensional
        structure, which can prevent the enzyme from carrying out its function.
        However, a decrease in enzyme activity does not always mean that the
        enzyme has been completely denatured. Smaller changes in active-site
        structure or amino-acid charge can also reduce activity.
    </p>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>

    <p>
        pH is an important factor that affects enzyme activity by changing the
        chemical properties and structure of enzymes. Changes in pH can alter
        the protonation and charge of amino-acid side chains, which can affect
        interactions within the enzyme and the shape of its active site. These
        changes can influence substrate binding and the rate of the reaction.
    </p>

    <p>
        Different enzymes have different optimum pH values because their
        structures and active sites are different. While smaller changes in pH
        may alter enzyme activity, extreme pH can disrupt the interactions that
        maintain protein structure and may lead to denaturation.
    </p>

    <p>
        Overall, the effect of pH on enzyme activity is closely linked to the
        relationship between amino-acid chemistry, protein structure, substrate
        binding and enzyme catalysis.
    </p>
